// A HOIST MAY NOT ESCAPE A SHORT-CIRCUIT GUARD. `matched && a.index(i)
// == b.index(j)` runs the LEFT index only while `matched` holds -- and
// `str.index` TRAPS past the end, which is how the type says "bug". So
// when the loop below walks one byte PAST `hay`'s length, the guard
// must say no first, and nothing on its right may run.
//
// The first #737 patch held an operator's left operand whenever either
// side contained a call. Holding HOISTS STATEMENTS (`value_held` ->
// `spill_valued`), and an `&&` lowered as plain C infix runs its right
// side under C's own `&&` but its LEFT beside the guard: the hoisted
// left index executed on every pass, hit the out-of-range byte, and
// trapped -- three `corpus/lsp/*` went red in parity while this file's
// shape hid inside them. Now a logical operator whose operands hold is
// written through the if-form instead, where the left fills its
// temporary ahead of the branch (Zen runs it there too) and the right's
// statements stay INSIDE it.
//
// THE WALK IS LSP'S OWN (`lsp_json_read.zen:word`, `matched = matched
// && self.text.index(from + i) == spelling.index(i)`): a prefix test
// over a range one LONGER than the needle, so on the last pass the
// needle side is out of range and only the guard stands between the
// program and a trap.
//
// MUST PRINT exactly:
// matched false
//
// An unguarded hoist traps here with "trap: integer overflow" from
// str.index's bound check; a backend that also broke the ORDER would
// print true.

str = std.text

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, AllocError> {
    hay     ::= "abcde";
    matched ::= hay.index(0) == hay.index(1);
    Range(0, 6).loop((h, i) {
        matched = matched && hay.index(i) == hay.index(i + 1);
    });
    println("matched {}", matched);
    Ok(0);
}
